  "meta": {
    "title": "Equal numbers of a’s and b’s, in any order",
    "blurb": "The stack holds the surplus: A’s while a is ahead, B’s while b is. A letter that matches the surplus grows it, the other cancels one. Deterministic, and accepted by final state whenever the surplus is zero.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "ε"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "ba",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbba",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbabaa",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "a",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "aab",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "babbb",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "counting",
        "deterministic-cfl",
        "textbook"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "The difference #a − #b is an integer that can go negative, and a stack can only hold a count that is at least zero. The trick is to store the **sign** in the stack symbol: a stack of A's means a is ahead by that many, a stack of B's means b is.\n\nThe \"check\" state is the only subtle part. After a cancelling letter, the machine needs to know whether the surplus just reached zero, and a deterministic PDA cannot both read a letter and test for Z in the same move. So it pops, then looks at what surfaced with an ε-move — which is allowed because no other move leaves *check*.\n\nCompare **#a − #b divisible by 3** among the DFAs: counting modulo a number is finite memory, counting exactly is not."
    }
  }
}
